The network takes two pictures of 960x576 and a phase between 0 and 1, and
gives back the picture in between. MacLC scales whatever it is playing to that
size and scales the result back.

Why 960x576 and not the full 1920x1088: measured on an M3 Max, one 1920x1088
inference costs about 30 ms on the GPU, and a 24 fps film doubled to 48 leaves
41.7 ms for the whole pass, conversions included -- so the full-size network
does not fit and the budget guard throws it out. At 960x576 the same network
costs about 11 ms and fits with room to spare. The in-between pictures are
therefore synthesised at that size and scaled up; they are shown for a
fiftieth of a second between two real ones.

A full-size model can be built with the converter in .agents/tools/rife-coreml
and pointed at with the "RIFE model" setting, on a machine fast enough for it.
